Marketing Strategies for Each Customer Segment 
🟦 Cluster 0 — Mid‑Income, Mid‑Spending (Practical Shoppers)
Profile:

Moderate annual income

Moderate spending score

Balanced, practical, value‑oriented

Not strongly brand‑loyal

Marketing Strategy:

Promote value bundles and mid‑range products

Use cross‑selling at checkout (e.g., “Frequently Bought Together”)

Offer loyalty points to encourage repeat visits

Send personalized email recommendations based on browsing history

Provide free samples to increase product trial

🟩 Cluster 1 — High Income, High Spending (Premium Shoppers)
Profile:

Highest income group

High spending score

Strong purchasing power

Likely to buy luxury or premium items

Marketing Strategy:

Offer VIP memberships, exclusive deals, and early access

Invite them to private shopping events

Provide personalized concierge support

Promote premium brands and limited‑edition products

Use high‑quality visuals and premium packaging

🟧 Cluster 2 — Low Income, Low Spending (Budget‑Conscious Shoppers)
Profile:

Lowest income group

Low spending score

Highly price‑sensitive

Focus on essentials

Marketing Strategy:

Promote discounts, coupons, and clearance sales

Offer budget bundles and essential‑only product lines

Use SMS/email alerts for price drops

Provide referral bonuses to encourage word‑of‑mouth

Highlight affordability and durability

🟪 Cluster 3 — Mid‑Income, Low Spending (Cautious Buyers)
Profile:

Moderate income

Low spending score

Careful, research‑driven shoppers

Prefer value over impulse purchases

Marketing Strategy:

Provide product comparison guides

Highlight long‑term value and warranties

Use educational content (blogs, demos, reviews)

Offer small loyalty rewards to encourage repeat purchases

Promote “Buy More, Save More” deals

🟨 Cluster 4 — High Income, Moderate Spending (Potential Premium Upsell Group)
Profile:

High income

Moderate spending score

Strong potential to increase spending

Open to premium products but not yet fully engaged

Marketing Strategy:

Use targeted upselling (“Upgrade for only $X more”)

Promote premium‑lite products (mid‑luxury tier)

Offer personalized recommendations based on browsing

Provide exclusive previews to increase brand attachment

Use email drip campaigns to nurture them into premium buyers

